## Add Flux on Head Node

The following command adds the {{ scheduler_name }} framework to the chosen
*head node*: `flux-core` (the message broker and job manager), `flux-sched` (the
Fluxion graph-based scheduler), `flux-security` (the job request signing
library and the setuid IMP that starts job processes as the submitting user)
and `flux-pmix` (PMIx support, which OpenMPI jobs need to bootstrap under Flux).
The latter also installs `pmix-ohpc` under `/opt/ohpc`, which the compute nodes
mount from the head node.
Note that the components needed by the compute nodes will be added to the
corresponding compute image in a subsequent step. Flux runs as a *system
instance*: one Flux broker per node, all connected in a tree-based overlay
network rooted at the head node. Note that Flux leverages the use of the *munge*
library to sign job requests and this daemon also needs to be running on all
hosts within the resource management pool.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Add resource management services on head node -->
```bash
# Install Flux server meta-package
{{ pkg_install }} ohpc-flux-server
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

The connections between the Flux brokers are authenticated and encrypted using a
CURVE certificate that has to be identical on all nodes and must only be
readable by the `flux` user. The certificate is generated as the `flux` user and
then moved into place, as `flux` has no write access to `/etc/flux/system`:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Create CURVE certificate for the Flux overlay network -->
```bash
runuser -u flux -- flux keygen /tmp/curve.cert
mv /tmp/curve.cert /etc/flux/system/curve.cert
chown flux:flux /etc/flux/system/curve.cert
chmod 0400 /etc/flux/system/curve.cert
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Next, configure the {{ scheduler_name }} system instance. The `bootstrap` table
lists all hosts in rank order: the *head node* is rank 0 and the parent of all
compute nodes, which connect to it on TCP port 8050. The `exec` and `access`
tables let users other than the `flux` instance owner run jobs through the IMP,
and the `resource` table withholds the *head node* (rank 0) from scheduling. Since the
same configuration is used on all nodes, it will also be made available to the
compute nodes in a subsequent step.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Configure the Flux system instance -->
```bash
cat > /etc/flux/system/conf.d/system.toml <<EOF
# Path to the IMP used to start jobs as the submitting user
[exec]
imp = "/usr/libexec/flux/flux-imp"

# Allow users other than the instance owner to run jobs, and give root
# owner privileges for administration
[access]
allow-guest-user = true
allow-root-owner = true

# Overlay network: hosts in rank order; the head node is rank 0 and the
# parent of all compute nodes
[bootstrap]
curve_cert = "/etc/flux/system/curve.cert"

[[bootstrap.hosts]]
host = "${sms_name}"
bind = "tcp://${sms_ip}:8050"
connect = "tcp://${sms_ip}:8050"

[[bootstrap.hosts]]
host = "${compute_prefix}[1-${num_computes}]"

# Do not schedule jobs on the head node (rank 0). The rank is used
# instead of the hostname, as hostnames cannot be mapped to ranks
# before the resources of all nodes have been discovered.
[resource]
exclude = "0"
EOF
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Finally, configure the IMP. As the only part of Flux that runs with root
privileges, it has its own configuration, which has to be owned by `root` and
must not be writable by other users. It only allows the `flux` user to start
jobs on behalf of the submitting user, and only with the installed Flux job
shell:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Configure the Flux IMP -->
```bash
cat > /etc/flux/imp/conf.d/imp.toml <<'EOF'
[exec]
allowed-users = [ "flux" ]
allowed-shells = [ "/usr/libexec/flux/flux-shell" ]
EOF
chmod 0644 /etc/flux/imp/conf.d/imp.toml
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

::: {.tip}
**Note:** Each `host` entry in the `bootstrap` table must exactly match the
output of the `hostname` command on that node, which is how each Flux broker
determines its own rank. With the configuration above, the resources of each
compute node are discovered via hwloc as its broker joins the instance, and the
full resource inventory is only defined once all compute nodes have joined.
Production systems will typically describe their resources statically instead,
using `[[resource.config]]` entries or an R file generated with `flux R`, so
that jobs can be scheduled even while some nodes are down. Changes to the
`bootstrap` table only take effect after restarting Flux on all nodes. Please
refer to the [Flux Administrator's
Guide](https://flux-framework.readthedocs.io/projects/flux-core/en/latest/guide/admin.html)
and `flux-config-resource(5)` for more detailed information.
:::
